<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=hwacha">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Weapons and war</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hwacha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1409">1409</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as hwach'a</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The hwacha was a Korean weapons platform, a wooden cart carrying a launch pad, first built in 1409 under the Joseon dynasty; it could fire up to 200 rocket-powered arrows, or volleys of arrows and bolts from gun barrels. It was developed from the juhwa and the singijeon, Korean rockets and rocket-powered arrows. Hundreds were deployed across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> by the end of 1451, and in the Japanese invasions of 1592–1598 forty hwachas helped 3,400 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koreans">Koreans</a> repel 30,000 Japanese at the Battle of Haengju.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hwacha.jpg" alt="Hwacha" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: draq · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hwacha2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Goryeo military leaders came to see firearms as necessary against the Japanese raiders who plundered coastal towns in growing numbers from 1350. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">Gunpowder</a> was imported from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1374, but the Chinese kept the knowledge of making it under strict embargo. Ch'oe Musŏn learned to purify potassium nitrate from visiting Chinese merchants, and a government office for gunpowder and firearms was set up in 1377 with him at its head, producing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hand-cannon">hand cannons</a> and rockets such as the juhwa.</p><p>The first hwacha was made by several scientists, among them Yi To and Ch'oe Haesan. In 1451 King Munjong ordered more powerful types, and by the end of that year fifty stood in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Hanseong</a>, present-day Seoul, and another 80 on the northern border.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The name means fire cart. The machine resembled a handcart whose top held a wooden launchpad of 100 to 200 holes for igniters; two arms at the back let the operator push and pull it, and tar oil eased its wheels on their iron axles. Its standard arrow was 1.1 m long, with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a>-filled paper tube below the head, and reached up to 2,000 m. Because it fired light arrows or small iron balls, it was far lighter and easier to move than the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> of contemporary Western warfare.</p><p>The Munjong hwacha, said to have been designed by the king and his younger brother Pe, had interchangeable modules: one fired 100 rocket-powered arrows, another 200 darts from 50 Chongtong gun barrels. The box-shaped Mangam hwacha carried forty barrels of 15 projectiles each, up to 600 bullets a salvo, and was worked by two soldiers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Operators primed each hole with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> from a bag tied to its igniter, loaded the ammunition, stepped back, covered their ears and pulled the igniter ropes. Against the Japanese invasions of the 1590s the weapons were mostly placed in fortresses and used defensively, and the dense formations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> infantry made good targets. At sea, on Panokseon warships, crews fired from the rowers' deck or aimed from the main deck at enemy sails. Some East Asian historians believe the hwacha and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turtle-ship">turtle ship</a> had a decisive effect on the war.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hwacha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
